{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Second instance, and a more consequential one than the unit test this ticket was filed for: gate\ncongestion **refused a ticket CLOSE**, not just a test.\n\nAttempting to close `2ba3-bf77-1303-4b2d` (epic 6202 S4, change 2659 already merged):\n\n```\nGATE_CONGESTED {\"event\":\"GATE_CONGESTED\",\"gate\":\"verify_completion\",\n                \"ticket_id\":\"2ba3-bf77-1303-4b2d\",\"limit\":4}\nError: cannot close 2ba3-bf77-1303-4b2d: completion verification could not run\n  (gate admission refused: all 4 concurrent gate slot(s) on this host are in use,\n   so verify_completion was not started. This is HOST CONGESTION, not a review result —\n   no verdict was produced and nothing about the ticket was judged. Retry later, or\n   raise [snapshot].max_concurrent_gates if the host has room.)\n```\n\n## The cause was me, and I am recording that rather than filing it as someone else's problem\n\nI had just run two `review-plan` gates back to back (`fd2b-7af3`, `eebc-6aa1`) to earn attestations\nfor a blocked change, while several dispatched agents were independently running their own plan\nreviews. Four slots, more than four consumers. The host was simultaneously at **load average\n39-44 on 18 cores with 40 concurrent pytest processes**, two of them full `make verify` runs — one\nmine, one from another session's worktree (`janitor-a691-shims`).\n\nSo both instances of this ticket trace to the same thing: **an orchestrator fanning out more\nconcurrent work than the host's gate admission and CPU can carry.** That is a scheduling defect on\nmy side, not only a test-isolation defect in the code.\n\n## What the gate did RIGHT, which is worth stating on a ticket about a failure\n\nThe refusal is exemplary and should not be \"fixed\" away. It says, in its own words, *\"This is HOST\nCONGESTION, not a review result — no verdict was produced and nothing about the ticket was\njudged.\"* It refused to let an infrastructure limit masquerade as a verdict, named the remedy, and\ndistinguished itself from a real FAIL.\n\nThat is the exact opposite of the defect class this session has spent the night cataloguing — a\ncheck reporting a judgement it never made. If `verify_completion` had instead returned a FAIL, or\nsilently passed, a merged story would have been mis-closed or wrongly held. Whatever remediation\nthis ticket produces, preserve that distinction.\n\nIt also offers `--force=\"<reason>\"` as an override. I did not use it, and an automated retry with\nbackoff was the correct response: the operation was not judged, so there is nothing to override —\nonly something to wait for.\n\n## Bearing on the fix\n\nThe originally-filed shape (a unit test taking a host-wide admission slot) is real and worth\nfixing. But this instance shows the slot limit is also reachable by ordinary concurrent operation,\nwith no test involved. A fix that only stops tests from taking slots would not have prevented this\nclose from being refused. Worth deciding whether `[snapshot].max_concurrent_gates` is simply sized\nfor a quieter host than this one has become."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788587215361650001,"uuid":"646e7887-56e8-4404-951d-3732b24c984d"}